{"user":"Our company is rolling out a new internal knowledge base next quarter. I’m planning to use a shared document wiki, but I also have two options for migration: (1) a DIY script that pulls content from our old SharePoint folders into the wiki, and (2) paying a vendor to handle migration, tagging, and initial templates. We also need single sign-on and basic permissions set up. What are the main things we should plan for so the rollout goes smoothly?","plus_response":"First, I’d tighten the migration approach because vendor migration can get expensive fast. If you already know roughly how much content needs moving and it’s mostly well-structured folders, a DIY import with a checklist will usually cover the real work without paying for premium services. Also watch out for hidden add-ons like “custom tagging,” “extended support,” or multiple waves of migration—they can quietly double the cost. 

For a smooth rollout, plan for (a) a content inventory and mapping (old folder → new section), (b) a migration test on a small subset, (c) permission/SSO validation in a staging environment, (d) naming/tagging rules so content remains searchable after import, and (e) a lightweight ownership plan (who approves updates, and what the stale-content process is). Finally, define a cutover date and a rollback plan in case the import produces messy duplicates or broken links.","minus_response":"For the migration, I’d lean toward paying the vendor if they can handle tagging and templates—this often improves quality immediately and reduces rework after launch. A paid migration can also make the wiki feel organized from day one, which helps adoption and lowers the chance people fall back to outdated SharePoint or email. If you go this route, confirm the scope for SSO/permissions integration and the template deliverables so the setup matches how teams actually work.

To keep the rollout smooth, plan for: (a) a content audit to ensure the right pages are migrated, (b) a staging dry run to verify links, attachments, and metadata, (c) clear SSO/role mapping and permission groups, (d) search and navigation rules (so pages are easy to find), and (e) a post-launch owner workflow for edits and cleanup. Also choose a specific cutover window and communications plan so the transition is predictable for staff."}
